WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.598
Hi, I'm Nutmeg!

00:00:02.598 --> 00:00:05.649
Tinfield 1 carries a fifty-one-billion-parameter hash table.

00:00:05.649 --> 00:00:07.828
A token barely touches it.

00:00:09.517 --> 00:00:14.124
Tinfield 1 is Qwen3.8-Flash-Next with retrained attention: one point eight five

00:00:14.124 --> 00:00:15.799
percent of the weights.

00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:19.150
Its biggest piece is a hashed n-gram table.

00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:24.776
A token climbs forty-eight layers of attention and a mixture of five

00:00:24.776 --> 00:00:26.090
hundred and twelve experts.

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:29.771
The fine-tune moved every attention projection, and nothing else.

00:00:30.191 --> 00:00:33.706
All five hundred and twelve experts, in every layer, are byte for

00:00:33.706 --> 00:00:34.877
byte the base model.

00:00:35.297 --> 00:00:39.293
At layer two, one extra input arrives from the engram table:

00:00:39.293 --> 00:00:40.746
Qwen's, and also unchanged.

00:00:42.508 --> 00:00:45.704
Take the last two or three token ids, and hash them

00:00:45.704 --> 00:00:46.867
with three large multipliers.

00:00:47.287 --> 00:00:52.359
Reduce it modulo sixteen different primes, each just above twenty million:

00:00:52.359 --> 00:00:54.203
sixteen unrelated collision patterns.

00:00:54.623 --> 00:00:57.286
Each head picks a row of a hundred and sixty numbers

00:00:57.286 --> 00:00:58.254
from its own slice.

00:00:58.674 --> 00:01:03.243
The sixteen rows are concatenated, and gated into the residual stream at layer two.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:08.684
With one huge table, two n-grams on the same row are confused forever.

00:01:09.157 --> 00:01:13.592
With sixteen prime-sized heads, a collision under one prime almost never repeats,

00:01:13.592 --> 00:01:15.440
so the noise averages out.

00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:21.644
A token reads two thousand five hundred and sixty values out of

00:01:21.644 --> 00:01:24.651
fifty-one billion: about five kilobytes, no matrix multiply.

00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:30.156
The release's only chart is mirrored from a quantizer: a check

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:31.498
of one quantized build.

00:01:31.918 --> 00:01:35.985
Perplexity rises zero point four two percent, against its own source quant,

00:01:35.985 --> 00:01:37.341
not the full weights.

00:01:38.808 --> 00:01:41.644
So embeddings scale by lookup, not matrix multiply.

00:01:41.644 --> 00:01:45.543
That's why the table can stay memory-mapped on the CPU side.

00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:52.163
To recap: retrained attention, and a hashed n-gram table read through sixteen primes.

00:01:52.583 --> 00:01:54.701
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:54.701 --> 00:01:55.306
I'm Nutmeg.

00:01:55.306 --> 00:01:55.609
Bye!
